{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 15, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "*highlighted section part 1*  \nRaymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was born in 1140 and was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His early life was marked by political instability in the Crusader states. Raymond inherited the county at a young age and his mother acted as regent until he came of age. During this time the County of Tripoli was under constant threat from Muslim forces and internal rivalries among the nobility. Raymond proved to be a cautious and diplomatic leader who preferred negotiation over confrontation. He was known for his ability to maintain alliances with neighboring Christian territories and to navigate the complex politics of the Levant.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*  \nRaymond III became a key figure in the Kingdom of Jerusalem after the death of King Baldwin IV in 1185. He was named regent for the young King Baldwin V who was only a child. Raymond faced intense opposition from Guy of Lusignan who sought to claim the throne. Raymond tried to maintain stability but his authority was undermined by factional infighting. His relationship with the military orders such as the Knights Templar and the Knights Hospitaller was strained. He was accused of being too passive and overly cautious by many of his contemporaries. Despite his efforts he failed to prevent the rise of Guy who eventually seized power after Baldwin V died. Raymond was sidelined and lost much of his influence in the final years of his life.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*  \nRaymond III died in 1187 shortly after the disastrous Battle of Hattin. He had been wounded in battle and was captured by Saladin. He was held for a short time and then released but his health never recovered. His death marked the end of an era of cautious diplomacy in the Crusader states. Raymond was remembered as a man of integrity who sought peace but was overwhelmed by the forces of war and ambition. His legacy is debated by historians. Some see him as a prudent leader who tried to preserve what he could while others view him as indecisive and ineffective. He was succeeded by his son Raymond IV who continued the family line in Tripoli. Raymond III's life reflects the decline of the Crusader states as internal divisions and external pressure led to their eventual collapse.", "logprobs": [{"token": "*", "logprob": -3.111314072157256e-05}, {"token": "highlight", "logprob": -0.0024852838832885027}, {"token": "ed", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " section", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " part", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "*", "logprob": -3.71926071238704e-05}, {"token": "  \n", "logprob": -0.03804221749305725}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -9.417489309271332e-06}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.008450583554804325}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.014097276143729687}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.09045735746622086}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -1.0609570381348021e-05}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0009839460253715515}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.00025376438861712813}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -3.135155202471651e-05}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.311301275563892e-06}, {"token": " until", "logprob": -0.47409361600875854}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -0.0026736250147223473}, {"token": " death", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -1.2993727978027891e-05}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.5497195136049413e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "8", "logprob"